- How do you optimize workflow performance?
There are several levers to make GitHub Actions fast and cost‑efficient:


Cache dependencies with actions/cache (for example ~/.npm, ~/.cache/pip) so repeat builds avoid reinstalling everything.
Run jobs in parallel when they don’t depend on each other (for example lint, test, security-scan as separate jobs).
Use matrix strategy to fan out tests across OS / language versions from a single job definition, instead of duplicating YAML.
Enable Docker layer caching or push pre‑built base images so Docker builds reuse layers efficiently.
Use paths filters to only run heavy workflows when relevant files change (for example skip backend tests on pure frontend change).
For very heavy pipelines, consider self-hosted runners or larger runner sizes to speed builds and reduce queue time.
The idea is to fail fast, avoid repeated work, and only run what’s necessary for the change set.

- What’s the difference between artifacts and cache?
Both artifacts and cache move data around, but they serve different purposes:

Artifacts


Outputs you want to persist or share between jobs in the same workflow run.
Examples: test reports, coverage files, build bundles, screenshots, SARIF security reports.
They are uploaded with actions/upload-artifact and can be downloaded by later jobs or humans after the run.
Cache


Data that speeds up future workflow runs by reusing dependencies or build outputs keyed by a hash.
Examples: node_modules, Maven/Gradle caches, ~/.cache/pip, large downloaded toolchains.
Implemented with actions/cache, keyed by things like OS + lockfile hash.
A useful way to phrase it: artifacts are reliable deliverables for this run; cache is best‑effort acceleration for future runs.
